1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,400
Let's create our fact table fact reviews and make an incremental materialization out of it

2
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:12,445
Here we have SLC reviews which you are already familiar with

3
00:00:12,445 --> 00:00:18,396
which has the listing ID review date reviewer name reviewer class and also the sentiment

4
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,990
Now we will do now two steps in the same model

5
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,000
First a little bit of cleansing

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,488
We want to make sure that only those reviews get to our fact table

7
00:00:28,488 --> 00:00:33,400
where there is an actual review text in them

8
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:38,933
And also we want to make sure that this table will be updated incrementally

9
00:00:38,933 --> 00:00:43,040
and not recreated at every dbt run

10
00:00:43,040 --> 00:00:46,760
So let's go and execute this

11
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:51,809
So here I will come to my project folder

12
00:00:51,809 --> 00:00:57,317
and let's create a new folder called fct

13
00:00:57,320 --> 00:01:06,592
I will put my fact tables here and this will be called fact reviews dot sql

14
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:13,954
Okay so first let's create a standard table without any increments

15
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:21,433
I will copy paste this and you will find this in the lessons resources

16
00:01:21,440 --> 00:01:23,600
So this is what you would do right

17
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:28,118
Normally you would say SLC reviews is a reference to SLC reviews

18
00:01:28,118 --> 00:01:34,393
and then just select from SLC reviews where the review text is not null

19
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:37,836
That would be a proper view materialization

20
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:42,675
Now what we want it to be is an incremental materialization

21
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:51,200
And for incremental materializations you can specify a configuration on the top of your file

22
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:54,980
If you want to do this you have a ginger tag for this

23
00:01:54,980 --> 00:01:58,760
and a built in dbt function called config

24
00:01:58,760 --> 00:02:06,800
So we create a template tag and we say we want to pass a model level configuration

25
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:09,486
The materialization should be incremental

26
00:02:09,486 --> 00:02:15,198
and we also need to specify what should happen if the schema changes

27
00:02:15,200 --> 00:02:21,647
Now we need to specify this because until now all the views and tables were recreated

28
00:02:21,660 --> 00:02:23,958
every time we executed dbt run

29
00:02:23,960 --> 00:02:30,267
But now we will have a table this factory views table that's already there

30
00:02:30,267 --> 00:02:32,200
and data should be appended

31
00:02:32,200 --> 00:02:36,313
So if upstream source tables change in schema

32
00:02:36,313 --> 00:02:41,920
so my select changes in schema we need to react on this somehow

33
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:49,396
So we will tell dbt to simply fail if there are any fishy changes out there

34
00:02:49,400 --> 00:02:52,580
So this is now an incremental materialization

35
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:56,757
But I also need to tell dbt how to increment

36
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:59,951
I need to tell dbt what are the new records

37
00:02:59,951 --> 00:03:04,680
how it knows from record that it's new or not

38
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:11,107
And here I can go and simply create a ginger if statement for this

39
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:20,072
I can say if this is an incremental load then append this SQL condition

40
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:26,958
And the SQL condition says that I'm only interested on those records

41
00:03:26,958 --> 00:03:40,440
where the review date from the original table is more current than our latest review date in this table

42
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:48,075
So here this refers to this model factory views

43
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:52,850
Now notice for a second how much freedom this leaves you

44
00:03:52,850 --> 00:03:55,394
because here if you want to have

45
00:03:55,400 --> 00:04:00,041
some more sophisticated logic like working on an updated field

46
00:04:00,041 --> 00:04:02,074
or anything doing with IDs

47
00:04:02,074 --> 00:04:07,594
whatever you're free to express it here in simple SQL

48
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:11,960
So that's why dbt is a great tool

49
00:04:11,960 --> 00:04:16,283
It's just so simple pieces put together

50
00:04:16,283 --> 00:04:20,319
giving you so much freedom All right

51
